# Rules of Engagement

This project rule **overrides** any default to “run commands / verify tests yourself.”

## The user runs ALL tests

Never run `pytest`, `python manage.py test`, or any other test runner unless the user **explicitly** says to run tests.

After Red **and** after Green: stop and ask the user to run the tests. Wait for their result before the next step.

```text
# ❌ BAD — agent verifies
pytest tests/test_health.py

# ✅ GOOD — agent implements or writes tests, then stops
# Ask: run pytest and tell me if it failed (Red) or passed (Green).
```

## Strict TDD

Write failing tests (Red) BEFORE any implementation code (Green). Do not implement production logic in the same step as the tests.

## Pause for Verification

After generating tests, stop. Do not write the implementation until the user confirms the tests fail as expected.

After implementing, stop. Do not run the suite. Do not assume Green until the user confirms the tests pass.

## Minimal Implementation

When implementing, write only the code required to make the failing tests pass. No extra features, refactors, or speculative helpers.

## Mock External APIs

Never make live network calls in tests. Mock the ESPN API with `unittest.mock` or `responses`.

```python
# ❌ BAD — live ESPN call in a test
def test_scoreboard():
    data = requests.get("https://site.api.espn.com/...")

# ✅ GOOD — mock the HTTP layer
@patch("mymodule.requests.get")
def test_scoreboard(mock_get):
    mock_get.return_value.json.return_value = {"events": []}
    ...
```
